 **Parameters:**
-- **Overlap Size:** Small number of slices (e.g., 1-2) sufficient to cover the rendering kernel's footprint [S2, unofficial].
-- **Sewing:** Optional; disabled if output is not a mesh [S1].
+- $S$: Total number of slices.
+- $P$: Number of processors.
+- $w_{overlap}$: Width of the overlap region in slices. Typical value: 1–2 slices [S2, unofficial].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Boundary Artifacts:** Naive non-overlapping division causes visual artifacts (black stripes) because opacity/color calculations are missing at the division boundaries [S2, unofficial].
-- **Load Imbalance:** If the volume has adaptive resolution, equal slice counts do not guarantee equal workload [S2, unofficial]. This method is designed for uniform resolution data.
-- **Index Mismatch:** In mesh sewing, vertex indices must be globally unique; failure to update indices leads to corrupted geometry [S1].
+- **Boundary Artifacts:** Naive non-overlapping division causes black stripes or discontinuities in compositing because opacity/color calculations are missing at the division boundaries [S2, unofficial].
+- **Load Imbalance:** If the volume has adaptive resolution, equal slice distribution may lead to workload imbalance. This method is designed for uniform resolution data [S2, unofficial].
+- **File Seeking:** Efficiency depends on slice order matching storage order. If not, seeking becomes expensive [S2, unofficial].
 
